  <h1>Is my dog overweight? An honest guide to dog weight</h1>
  <p class="guide-lede">Extra weight is the most common — and most fixable — health problem dogs have, and the kindest-feeling habits cause it. Lean dogs live longer, move better, and cost less at the vet. Here's how to see your dog honestly and fix it without misery.</p>

  <h2>How do I tell if my dog is overweight?</h2>
  <p>Three checks, no scale needed. <strong>Ribs:</strong> resting your hands flat on your dog's sides, you should feel ribs under a light layer — like the back of your hand, not like a padded armrest. <strong>Waist:</strong> from above, there should be a visible tuck behind the ribs. <strong>Profile:</strong> from the side, the belly should rise toward the hips, not hang level. Fail two of three and your dog is carrying extra — like most dogs, so no shame, just fix it.</p>

  <h2>Why does it matter so much?</h2>
  <p>Because weight touches everything: joints load with every extra pound, hearts work harder, and lean-fed dogs in long-term research lived meaningfully longer with later onset of arthritis than their heavier littermates. If you do only one thing for an aging dog, this is the thing.</p>

  <h2>How do I get the weight off?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Measure the food. Actually measure it.</strong> The scoop is not a measurement. Most overfeeding is an honest 20% pour error repeated twice a day for years.</li>
    <li><strong>Count the treats as food.</strong> Treats are calories with better marketing. Either budget them into the day's total or swap for carrot, green beans, or a piece of the regular kibble.</li>
    <li><strong>Feed the dog you want, not the dog on the bag.</strong> Bag portions are generous averages. Feed for target weight, reassess every two weeks by the rib check.</li>
    <li><strong>Walk it off together.</strong> Consistent moderate exercise keeps the muscle that makes weight loss stick — and it's the part your dog experiences as a bonus, not a diet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What about "weight management" foods?</h2>
  <p>Some genuinely help — higher protein to preserve muscle, more fiber so the dog feels full. Some just dilute the food with fillers so your dog eats the same volume, gets less nutrition, and stays hungry. The panel tells you which one you're holding: named protein first is the good kind.</p>

  <h2>When is it a vet problem?</h2>
  <p>Sudden weight gain or loss without a feeding change, a pot-bellied look with thin legs, or ravenous hunger that appeared from nowhere — those pattern like thyroid, Cushing's, or other medical causes. Get a workup before a diet.</p>

